<p>I have written elsewhere about the benefits - and thus the necessity - of a wealth tax.</p>
<p>The problem is that much wealth is easily transported and thus - without a global, harmonized wealth tax - it is impossible to ensure that wealth stays to be taxed.  Don't get me wrong: I would like to see a global wealth tax.</p>
<p>But in the short term, the British government can do more than wring their hands and the British public should demand that they do more.  Of course, the right wing media (particularly the Telegraph and Mail) will claim that nothing can be done or that it is an attack on "wealth creators".  It's nothing of the sort.</p>
<p>One obvious thing that is held (and abused) by people with wealth and that cannot be moved is land, so it makes sense to tax it.  What does not make sense is to tax all land under all uses at the same rate.  Some uses are more valuable than others and should be taxed accordingly.  Your opinions will doubtless differ from mine, and any actual legislation would only be the start of an evolving tax.  But these are the broad categories into which I would break land use down.  The tax would be a variable amount based on a base rate, which, for some reason is called millage in a number of contexts.  I will therefore set the base rate, or unit, at 1mill.</p>
<p>The highest rate of tax would apply to uses that I would classify as destructive, hurtful and useless.  The most obvious example would be car parks.  These are damaging to the environment (they are metalled and thus non-porous, and do not support any life), take up an excessive amount of space (especially flat car parks), could be put underground without loss of function (thus not using up <i>any</i> land) and do not really contribute to life at all.  I would set a millage rate of 10mills for land used by car parks.</p>
<p>The next rate of tax would go to similar land use which could easily be optimized.  Things like bus garages and sprawling road layouts come to mind.  These would all get a millage rate of 5mills.</p>
<p>All other roads and transport infrastructure, together with office buildings, factories and other "places of work" (as well as buildings such as churches) would come in for the next rate of 3mills.</p>
<p>All other property, which is basically residential property, would be taxed at 2mills.</p>
<p>For both offices and residential property, there is clearly a benefit to building up: an apartment block with 20 floors takes the same amount of land area as a house with the same footprint, but obviously has a significantly higher number of people sharing the tax payments, particularly if the surrounding area is <i>not</i> included as part of the building but is specifically designated (and maintained) as a wilderness area.</p>
<p>Farmland is probably the most contentious category and, moreover, there are a lot of things that I don't understand about farming.  My default would be to say that farmland is charged at 1mill, but I can see that a sliding scale of 0-2mills makes a lot of sense depending on the use and the incentives that the government might have in mind.  I would probably also put parkland and urban parks and green spaces somewhere in this category.  Private parks and areas would all could as residential property and be taxed at 2mills.</p>
<p>Finally, representing a credit of 0-1mills, owners of land have the opportunity to designate and maintain "wilderness" or "sustainable ecology" areas.  The idea here is that anyone who designates a sufficiently large area (at least one acre, I would say) to be a "wilderness" does not pay any tax.  Larger areas, particularly if they are actively managed wilderness areas, would be entitled to receive funding to support their maintenance of up to 1mill per acre.  It's important to note that this really does mean <i>wilderness</i>; a community park does not count.  For smaller areas (particularly in urban areas), in order to qualify for this rate it must be demonstrated that people are explicitly prohibited from entering these areas.  Larger areas may support recreational use, but that would have to be a very small percentage of the use: by and large, the purpose of this would be to create and encourage biodiversity.  The tax benefit is an <i>incentive</i>, not an opportunity for tax fraud.</p>
<p>The devil is always in the details and I recognize that there would have to be exceptions or at least variations; for example for historic buildings such as castles and churches.  A simple exception would be to say that any government organization preserving such buildings would not be liable for the tax.</p>
<p>To be clear, I do not see this tax as being revenue neutral or anything like it: it is both a way of raising money by taxing the wealthiest in society, and making people think about how best to utilize their land assets in a way which benefits the environment.  If the tax ever became close to revenue neutral, I would suspect that the incentives were wrong.</p>
<p>There is a case to be made that the wealthiest landowners, specifically those who own residential property to rent out, will merely "pass this tax along to the renters".  This is true, and no, there is nothing <i>directly</i> that can be done to stop them.  I discuss this problem and what <i>can</i> be done elsewhere.</p>
